# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
"""Tool write-ups (description field) for Quadrilaterals. Keyed by tool media id."""
DESCRIPTIONS = {
"M2.S2.T2.C6.2D1": (
"Two wooden strips lie across a workbench, AC and BD, crossing at a point O, with a loop of thread pulled tight round their four ends. "
"Below are a length slider for each strip, a joint slider that moves O along a strip, and a crossing angle slider. "
"Live read-outs show the four corners of the thread figure and its four sides. The job is the carpenter's: lay the strips so that the thread makes a rectangle.\n\n"
"Before touching a slider the student predicts whether the thread will make a rectangle. In the first card AC is 8 cm and the student sets the other strip and the joint. "
"A strip of the wrong length, or a joint off the middle, leaves corners that are not 90 degrees, with their values shown, so the failure is seen. "
"When the other strip is also 8 cm and both are joined at their midpoints, all four corners read 90. "
"Then the student turns the crossing angle. At 60 degrees the sides are 4 cm and about 6.93 cm. "
"At 140 degrees, with the four corners predicted first, the sides are about 7.52 cm and 2.74 cm, a long thin rectangle. "
"At 90 degrees the four sides are about 5.66 cm each and the tool names the figure a square, a rectangle with equal sides. "
"The angle changes the shape of the rectangle but never its corners.\n\n"
"The last two cards break the rule on purpose. Strips of 8 cm and 6 cm joined at their midpoints never give a rectangle: at 90 degrees the thread is a rhombus with sides of 5 cm and corners near 74 and 106 degrees. "
"Sliding the joint of an equal 8 cm strip to 2 cm from its end marks the halves of 2 cm and 6 cm as unequal, and at a 90 degree crossing the corners read about 127, 83, 67 and 83 degrees. "
"Hints ask what must be true of the two strips and of where they are joined, then suggest making them equal and joining them at their midpoints before trying angles, "
"then state that equal 8 cm strips joined at their midpoints give 90 degrees at 60, at 140 and at any angle.\n\n"
"The set is done once the rectangle has been made at three different crossing angles. The tool then notes that three measured angles only suggest the rule, and shows the working for any angle x: "
"one small isosceles triangle has base angles 90 minus x/2, the next has x/2, and each corner is their sum, 90. "
"Through doing, the student learns that two equal strips joined at their midpoints make a rectangle at every crossing angle, and that measuring suggests a rule while the working proves it."
),

"M2.S4.T4.C13.2D1": (
"A four-cornered figure is pinned on squared paper with a draggable handle on each corner. Equal sides carry matching hatch marks, equal angles matching arcs, and every right angle the small square. "
"A switch chooses the figure: a free quadrilateral, a constructed parallelogram or a constructed rectangle. "
"The student can lock one side, show or hide the readouts of the four sides, four angles and both diagonals, and reset the shape. "
"Beside the figure, hidden at first, is a panel of eight statements: opposite sides equal, opposite angles equal, next door angles adding to 180, diagonals bisecting each other, diagonals equal, "
"all four angles right angles, all four sides equal, and diagonals meeting at right angles. "
"Each has a tick that lights only while the statement is true at the position on screen, and a badge saying whether the construction keeps it.\n\n"
"With the panel hidden, the student starts with a parallelogram, predicts which measurements will stay equal while the corners move, and commits that guess. The panel then opens beside the guess. "
"Four statements read Kept: opposite sides equal, opposite angles equal, next door angles adding to 180 and diagonals bisecting. The other four read Not true now. "
"The student drags a corner through many positions while a counter records how many distinct positions have been tried. The four kept ticks never flicker. "
"In the one position where the parallelogram looks like a rectangle, equal diagonals and four right angles light up. The next drag switches them off, and the tool strikes them through with the position that broke them and says they were true here but not kept.\n\n"
"The same eight statements are then put to a free quadrilateral, where none survives, and to a constructed rectangle, which keeps six however it is tilted. "
"The cards ask, for example, whether equal diagonals should join the kept list when both readouts land on the same number, and whether a tally of 20 unbroken positions settles a property. "
"A rectangle with sides of 8 and 15 units shows two diagonals of 17, and a parallelogram with one angle of 55 degrees has a largest angle of 125. "
"A drag that would put three corners on one line, cross two sides or shrink a side to nothing is refused, with the reason drawn at the corner that caused it. "
"The student must also say which part of the construction forces a property; the answer that it looks equal is not accepted. "
"Hints ask what the construction fixes and what it leaves free, then suggest dragging to try to switch the statement off, then show the position that breaks it. "
"Through doing, the student learns that a property forced by how a figure is built survives every drag, while one that a single drag can switch off was only true of the drawn picture."
),

"M2.S6.T6.C22.2D1": (
"A square frame of side 8 cm has one point marked on each side, every point the same distance from its corner and all measured the same way round. "
"Joining the four points draws a shaded inner figure, with read-outs for its four sides, four angles and its area. "
"A slider sets the distance of all four points from their corners, a snap button puts them on the midpoints, and each point can also be dragged on its own.\n\n"
"The student first predicts what the inner figure is before moving the slider. Snapping to the midpoints gives a square with sides of about 5.66 cm, four right angles and an area of 32 square centimetres, and the card asks for its name. "
"Setting the distance to 2 cm gives a tilted square with sides of about 6.32 cm and an area of 40 square centimetres. "
"A wrong prediction shows the four corner triangles side by side, so the student sees that they are equal right triangles, with legs of 2 cm and 6 cm here. "
"Finding the distance that makes the area exactly 32 square centimetres leads back to 4 cm, the midpoints. "
"Another distance that gives the same inner square as 2 cm is 6 cm, which gives a square of the same size tilted the other way.\n\n"
"At the midpoints the student reads the two acute angles of a corner triangle, 45 degrees each, and the angle of the inner figure, 90 degrees. "
"At 2 cm the corner triangles have acute angles of about 18 and 72 degrees, yet the inner corners still read 90. "
"Finally the student drags one point alone off its place: the inner sides become unequal, the right angles go out, and the student says the figure is no longer a square.\n\n"
"Hints ask which four points are marked and how far each is from its corner, then ask whether the four corner triangles are equal and what their acute angles are, "
"then show that the two acute angles add to 90 degrees, so the angle left on the straight line is 180 minus 90, which is 90. "
"The set is done when the inner square has been made at three different distances. "
"Through doing, the student learns that equal corner triangles are the reason the inner figure is always a square, and that the midpoints are only one case of it."
),

"M3.S8.T8.C31.2D1": (
"A lettered quadrilateral ABCD sits on a working sheet beside an empty meeting point. Each of its four interior angles is drawn as a coloured wedge of the same radius, so the space a corner covers matches its size. "
"Before anything is torn the total is hidden, and the student predicts whether the four angles will fill the turn exactly, leave a gap or overlap.\n\n"
"The student taps a corner to tear its wedge off, leaving a dashed notch, and drags it to the meeting point, where it snaps edge to edge against what is already there. The wedge keeps its measure printed on it. "
"A running total climbs and the part of the turn still to be filled is drawn in a second colour. "
"After two wedges the total reads 180 with a half turn gap, the answer carried over from triangles, seen to be unfinished. When the other two wedges land the gap closes and the total reads 360. "
"The tool never closes a gap by itself: with corners of 88, 104 and 61 degrees placed, the gap reads 107 degrees, the size of the missing corner. "
"Then the student draws the diagonal that lies inside the figure and the two triangles are labelled 180 each. "
"Reshaping the quadrilateral puts the corners back on a different figure, so the tearing has to be done again while the diagonal argument has not changed, "
"and the student chooses which route proves it for every quadrilateral.\n\n"
"The student also bends one corner inward and finds the total still 360, with the inward corner read as a reflex angle. "
"A typed set such as 95, 95, 95 and 90 is refused before any figure is drawn, because it totals 375, which is 15 more than a full turn. "
"The practice cards find the fourth angle from 95, 85 and 110 degrees, which is 70, and the largest of four angles in the ratio 2 to 3 to 4 to 6, which is 144. "
"A student who says the angles add to 180 because a quadrilateral is made of triangles is shown that the diagonal makes two triangles and not one. "
"Through doing, the student learns that the four angles of any quadrilateral add to 360 degrees because a diagonal makes two triangles of 180, and that tearing shows one figure while the diagonal explains all of them."
),

"M4.S10.T10.C37.2D1": (
"One quadrilateral stands on a plain ground beside a checklist of its sides and angles. Equal sides share a colour and matching hatch marks, equal angles carry arcs of the same radius, "
"parallel pairs carry arrowheads, and every pair of angles that must add to 180 is joined by a linking arc printing the sum, such as 70 + 110 = 180. "
"A family selector offers parallelogram, rhombus, rectangle, square and trapezium, and a shape slider sweeps the figure from one end of its family to the other. "
"Each checklist row stays closed until the student has predicted it, for example whether next door angles in a parallelogram are equal or add to 180.\n\n"
"As the student sweeps the slider, every number on the figure changes but the same marks hold throughout: opposite sides equal, opposite angles equal, and each next door pair adding to 180. "
"A relation that holds at one value only, such as all four angles reading 90, is drawn dashed and captioned 'at this value only', and sweeping on turns it off. "
"This separates the pair of angles that are equal from the pair that add to 180. "
"Switching from parallelogram to rectangle, rhombus and square adds one condition at a time and the checklist grows without clearing. "
"In a rectangle the two relations give the same number because 90 is its own supplement, and the tool points out that they agree only there. "
"A trapezium carries linking arcs only on the pairs along its slanting sides, since the family forces nothing else.\n\n"
"The cards ask, for example, for the next door angle of a parallelogram with an angle of 62 degrees (118), the next door angle of a rhombus with an angle of 105 degrees (75), "
"the opposite angle of a parallelogram with an angle of 137 degrees (137), and the perimeters when sides are 13 and 9 (44) or a rhombus side is 17 (68). "
"Then the student proposes a smallest set of properties, for example one pair of equal opposite sides, and the tool draws a figure that meets the set but lies outside the family, showing the set is too weak. "
"Hints ask which pair of angles lies between the same two parallel sides, then suggest sweeping and watching which marks never go out, then name the pair that adds to 180. "
"Through doing, the student learns that a parallelogram forces equal opposite angles and next door angles adding to 180, and that the families differ by the relations they force and not by how they look."
),

"M4.S14.T14.C48.2D1": (
"Four equal rods are hinged at the corners of a rhombus ABCD. A handle at A opens and closes the hinge, and both diagonals are drawn meeting at O with a small right angle mark. "
"Live read-outs give the four corners and the angles each diagonal makes at each corner. A switch to diagonal mode gives a slider for each diagonal, and the rods then follow.\n\n"
"The student first predicts which marks will stay as the hinge opens. Opening the hinge to 50 degrees at A gives corners of 50, 130, 50 and 130, and the student reads the other three. "
"Opposite angles stay equal, neighbouring angles stay at 180, and the diagonal from A makes 25 degrees on each side. "
"The right angle mark at O never goes out while the diagonal lengths change, so the student sees the diagonals meet at 90 degrees at every opening. "
"In rhombus UVWX, setting the angle the diagonal makes at V to 30 degrees gives a corner of 30 plus 30, which is 60 degrees, with neighbouring corners of 120 degrees.\n\n"
"In diagonal mode, diagonals of 5 cm and 4 cm give a side of about 3.20 cm, and diagonals of 8 cm and 6 cm give a side of 5 cm and a perimeter of 20 cm, "
"because each side is the hypotenuse of a right triangle whose legs are half of each diagonal, 4 and 3. "
"Then the student opens the hinge to a square and sees the diagonals become equal while still meeting at 90 degrees. "
"The last card sweeps the hinge looking for any angle other than 90 at which the diagonals are equal. There is none, and the equal diagonals at 90 degrees are drawn dashed as belonging to that opening only.\n\n"
"Hints ask what stays the same as the hinge opens, the rods, the angles or the diagonals, then say each corner is twice the angle its diagonal makes, then work 30 plus 30 is 60 and 180 minus 60 is 120. "
"The set is done when the diagonals have been shown at right angles at every opening. "
"Through doing, the student learns that a rhombus keeps its equal sides and its perpendicular, bisecting diagonals as it changes shape, and that equal diagonals appear only when it becomes a square."
),

"M5.S16.T16.C56.2D1": (
"Two crossing segments, the diagonals AC and BD meeting at O, float alone on a plain ground with no figure drawn around them. "
"One tick marks each half of AC and two ticks each half of BD, drawn only where that diagonal really is cut in half, and a small square at O marks a right angle crossing. "
"Both lengths and the crossing angle are printed live along the top. Beside the diagonals sit three labelled conditions that light by themselves: bisect each other, equal in length, meet at right angles. "
"Under them the tool prints the whole set of families that the current conditions guarantee, not a single name.\n\n"
"The student first reads only the three conditions and names the quadrilateral the four endpoints will make, with the outline hidden. "
"With diagonals of 16 and 16 bisecting each other at an angle that is not 90 degrees, a first guess is often square, and the tool does not say. "
"Pressing reveal joins the endpoints in order and a rectangle appears, so the conditions are read back one at a time as the reason it had to come out that way. "
"Then the right angle lock is switched on, the outline becomes a square, and rectangle stays lit beside it: the earlier answer was a set all along. "
"Equal bisecting diagonals give a rectangle, and the square is only one case of it.\n\n"
"The student drags any endpoint, slides the crossing along a diagonal, and sets a target family. A failed target is answered by naming the missing condition, such as bisecting. "
"The cards ask, for example, for the pieces of a bisected diagonal of 24 units (12 each), the side of a figure with bisecting diagonals of 18 and 24 units at right angles (15, with Pythagoras said out loud), "
"14 and 48 (25), and the other diagonal of a rhombus of side 13 with one diagonal of 24 units (10). "
"A crossing dragged off one diagonal, or two diagonals dragged towards one straight line, is refused with the reason on screen, since neither describes a figure. "
"Hints ask which conditions are on and which are missing, then suggest turning them on one at a time, then name the family each combination guarantees. "
"Through doing, the student learns to use the diagonals as the test for a family: bisecting gives a parallelogram, equal lengths add a rectangle, a right angle crossing adds a rhombus, and all three give a square."
),

"M5.S18.T18.C62.2D1": (
"The diagonal AC is fixed at 6 cm with its midpoint O marked, and a line through O at right angles to AC crosses the screen. Two points B and D sit on that line and can be dragged. "
"A thread runs round A, B, C and D, hatch marks show which sides are equal, a right angle mark sits at O, and read-outs give the four sides, the lengths OB and OD, and the angles at B and D.\n\n"
"The student first predicts which sides will stay equal as B and D slide. With OB = 2 cm and OD = 6 cm the sides read AB = BC of about 3.61 cm and AD = CD of about 6.71 cm: "
"two pairs of equal neighbouring sides, a kite. Keeping BD at 8 cm, the student slides the split through 3 and 5, then 1 and 7. "
"The sides change, to about 4.24 and 5.83, then 3.16 and 7.62, but the two pairs of equal neighbouring sides, the right angle at O and AO = OC do not. "
"The student then checks which diagonal cuts which in half: BD always cuts AC in half at a right angle, while AC cuts BD in half only when OB and OD are equal.\n\n"
"Next B and D are placed the same distance from AC. With OB = OD = 4 cm the sides read 5 cm each, all four hatch marks match, and the figure is named a rhombus as well; the student reads the side. "
"With OB = OD = 3 cm the diagonals are both 6 cm and the figure is a square, which the tool names together with every other name it earns, such as rhombus, rectangle, parallelogram and kite.\n\n"
"Hints ask which diagonal stays fixed and which line B and D slide along, then say B and D are each the same distance from A and from C because they sit on the line that cuts AC in half at right angles, "
"then work AC = 6, OB = 2 and OD = 6 to show AB = BC and AD = CD. The set is done when the kite has been shown at three different positions of B and D. "
"Through doing, the student learns that a kite has two pairs of equal neighbouring sides, that one diagonal is the perpendicular bisector of the other, and that a kite is not always a rhombus."
),

"M5.S20.T20.C67.2D1": (
"Two parallel lines run across the screen, and a trapezium PQRS has its sides PQ and SR on them. A handle slides the top side SR along its line, and handles at P and Q tilt the slanting sides. "
"Read-outs show the four angles, a linking arc along each slanting side prints the sum of its two angles, and a running total adds all four. An equal slanting sides lock sits beside them.\n\n"
"The student sets the base angles at P and Q to 70 and 55 degrees and predicts the two top angles before reading them. "
"A wrong guess shows the linking arc with 180 along the slanting side and the angle that is missing, and the tool then shows 110 at S and 125 at R, because each slanting side is a transversal of the two parallel lines. "
"Given 105 and 135 degrees at the ends of one parallel side, the student finds the other two, 75 and 45, with the same arcs. "
"Adding the four angles of the 70 and 55 trapezium gives 70 + 55 + 110 + 125 = 360, which the student compares with the angle sum of a quadrilateral.\n\n"
"Switching the lock on keeps the slanting sides equal and makes the trapezium isosceles, so the angles at the ends of each parallel side match. "
"With one angle set to 100 degrees the four angles read 100, 100, 80 and 80, and with base angles of 65 degrees the top angles read 115. "
"With the lock off, the student slides the top side until the base angles are 70 and 110 degrees. Now the slanting sides are parallel, the figure is a parallelogram, "
"and the tool names it a trapezium as well, so the second name is not lost.\n\n"
"Hints ask which sides are parallel and which are slanting, then say a slanting side is a transversal so its two angles add up to 180, then give 180 minus 70 = 110. "
"The set is done once a parallelogram has been made from a trapezium and named as both. "
"Through doing, the student learns to find the angles of a trapezium without measuring, that equal slanting sides give equal base angles, and that a parallelogram is a trapezium too."
),

"M5.S22.T22.C71.2D1": (
"Nested rings are drawn on a plain ground: Quadrilaterals around Parallelograms, and inside those two overlapping lobes, Rectangles and Rhombuses, whose overlap is drawn as its own region labelled Squares. "
"Beneath the rings is a tray of figure cards: square, rectangle, rhombus, parallelogram, trapezium and kite. "
"Each card is a real quadrilateral with measured sides, angles and diagonals, and the number of cards in each region stays on screen.\n\n"
"The rings start empty and the student first predicts where a square belongs. Then cards are dragged onto rings. A card that belongs exactly there locks in. "
"A card that could go further in is accepted as true but incomplete, and the tool asks whether it could go further. "
"A card that fails a condition bounces back with the failed property and its measured value, for example an angle of 72 degrees where 90 was needed. "
"The square card is refused everywhere except the overlap: in the rectangles lobe it would have to leave the rhombuses, and its measured sides 7, 7, 7, 7 are printed beside it. "
"Once five cards, all but the square, are in their innermost rings the counts read Quadrilaterals 5, Parallelograms 3, Rectangles 1 and Rhombuses 1, so every card in an inner ring is counted in every ring around it. "
"The square lights five regions while the rectangle beside it lights three.\n\n"
"Tapping a card turns it over to show its measured sides, angles and diagonals grouped by the ring each one decides. Turning a card to any angle changes no region. "
"The student can also build a figure of their own on a snapped grid and watch the same conditions place it. "
"The cards ask, for example, how many of the 5 regions a card with sides 11, 4, 11, 4 and four angles of 90 belongs to (3), "
"and a rhombus card with sides of 6 and angles of 72 and 108 (3), and how many of the six tray cards belong in the rectangles region (2) and in the parallelograms region (4). "
"The kite and trapezium cards rest only in the outer ring. The student also states what a card must satisfy to move one ring further in, and the tool tests the claim. "
"Hints ask which conditions the card meets, then which ring needs one more condition, then show the measured row that decided the ring. "
"Through doing, the student learns that a figure belongs to every ring whose conditions it meets, that a square is a rectangle and a rhombus at once, and that measures, not appearance or turning, decide the region."
),
}
